gaid(){return"1. How the app works"},
gaic(){return"City, Country & More is a word game hosted on Android. A game can run locally on the same Wi-Fi network or over a hotspot. Since version 1.2.0 the host can also make a room available online, and other players can join through a compatible web client.\n\nThe app does not require an account, sign-in, or registration. We do not operate our own backend that stores game state or a database of active rooms. The Android host remains the source of truth for game state, rounds, answers, assessments, and scoring.\n\nIn online mode, the public PeerJS service is used for signaling needed to establish a WebRTC connection. Once connected, game data is sent directly between participants over a WebRTC DataChannel. The project does not use its own TURN server.\n\nThe app may also use the internet to display ads, manage advertising consent, and download remote ad configuration when ads are enabled in a build."},
gaif(){return"2. Data that may be processed"},
gaie(){return"Data provided by the user may include a player name or nickname, answers entered during a round, decisions and assessments related to answers, and app and game settings. We do not require a legal name, email address, or phone number.\n\nIn a local game, devices may exchange the player name and identifier, room code and identifier, host IP address and port, game state, categories, letter, answers, assessments, statistics, scores, and technical information needed to maintain the connection.\n\nIn online mode, additional data may be processed: the six-character room code and technical PeerJS session identifiers derived from it, connection identifiers and PeerJS signaling data, ICE and network information required by WebRTC, including IP addresses or other data needed to establish the connection, and reconnect data for the active session.\n\nThe app may store the player name, settings, active or most recent session data, data needed for reconnect, and a local answer draft on the device.\n\nThe production web client is a static site hosted by GitHub Pages. The hosting provider may process standard technical data from an internet request according to its own policies.\n\nWhen ads are enabled, Google Mobile Ads / AdMob and Google partners may process technical device and app-use data, including the advertising identifier, IP address, approximate location derived from IP, device and app information, and interactions with ads. The scope depends on configuration, region, and user consent."},
gaih(){return"3. Camera and QR codes"},
gaig(){return"The app may request camera access when scanning a QR code. The code may contain data needed to join a local or online room. The app does not save photos or recordings and does not send camera images to the publisher. QR scanning is optional."},
gaij(){return"4. Notifications and background operation"},
gaii(){return"During an active multiplayer game, the Android app may start a Foreground Service to keep the session active after the app is minimized. Android then shows a persistent notification about the active game. The service runs only during an active session and stops after leaving the room or ending the session."},
gail(){return"5. Ads and consent"},
gaik(){return"The app may display Google AdMob ads. They may be personalized or non-personalized depending on region, configuration, and consent. The app may use Google User Messaging Platform for the consent form and privacy options. If required conditions are not met, the app may not load ads or may display them in a limited form."},
gaio(){return"6. Remote ad configuration"},
gaim(){return"The app may download a public ad configuration over HTTPS. A server handling that address may receive standard technical connection data such as the IP address, request time, and HTTP client information, depending on the hosting configuration."},
gaiq(){return"7. Who data may be shared with"},
gaip(){return"Game data is shared with participants in the same room to the extent needed to play together. In online mode, technical signaling and connection data is processed with the public PeerJS service and the network infrastructure needed to establish WebRTC. The web client is delivered through GitHub Pages. Technical and advertising data may be processed by Google and its partners when ads are enabled. We do not sell users' personal data."},
gais(){return"8. Android permissions"},
gair(){return"The app may use INTERNET for local and online communication and network services, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE to check connectivity, CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE as a system requirement for the foreground service that maintains a network connection with other devices, CAMERA for optional QR scanning, POST_NOTIFICATIONS for the active-game notification, and FOREGROUND_SERVICE plus FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE to maintain an active multiplayer session in the background."},
gaiu(){return"9. Data storage and deletion"},
gait(){return"Data stored locally is kept for as long as needed for the app to work or until the user removes it, for example by clearing app data or uninstalling the app. Minimal data needed to reconnect or resume an unfinished session may remain locally after the process restarts. Retention of data processed by PeerJS Cloud, GitHub Pages, Google Mobile Ads, and other external services is governed by those services' policies."},
gai5(){return"10. Security"},
gai4(){return"In LAN/hotspot mode, communication may use a local cleartext WebSocket without TLS. Do not use a local game on an untrusted network if you do not want room or game data to be visible to people who have access to that network.\n\nWebRTC connections used online are encrypted by WebRTC transport mechanisms. Signaling uses the public PeerJS service. The project uses direct P2P and does not operate its own TURN server. Connections to the web client and remote configuration use HTTPS."},
gai7(){return"11. Children and families"},
gai6(){return"The app is a word game that may be used by players of different ages. We do not ask for contact details or require an account. Google Play configuration, target audiences, and advertising must match the actual published app variant and applicable Google Play requirements."},
gai9(){return"12. Your rights"},
gai8(){return"Depending on where the user lives, they may have rights to information about data processing, access, correction, deletion, restriction of processing, or objection. For privacy matters, contact the publisher through the contact details shown on Google Play or the public GitHub profile PawelWielga. Locally stored data can be removed by clearing app data or uninstalling the app."},
gaib(){return"13. Changes to this policy"},
gaia(){return"This policy may be updated when the app, multiplayer, scope of processed data, service providers, advertising configuration, or legal requirements change."},
